  ],
  "text": "We make more transistors each year than the world grows grains of rice. And almost all of the best ones come out of a handful of factories. You do not carve a chip. You print it, with light. A pattern is shone through a mask onto a wafer coated in something light sensitive. Then it is etched, coated and printed again. Dozens of times, layer on layer. To print smaller you need shorter wavelengths, and that is where it gets absurd. The machines make extreme ultraviolet by hitting falling tin droplets with a laser. Twice each. One machine costs hundreds of millions, weighs as much as a bus, and one company makes them. The room is cleaner than an operating theatre, because a single speck ruins the chip under it. Which is why a chip shortage is not a factory problem. It is a one supplier problem. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
